# ADR-0011: Use OpenSearch for Lexical Search

- **Status:** Accepted
- **Date:** 2026-07-13
- **Decision Makers:** Architecture Team

---

# Context

The Enterprise AI Orchestration Platform provides Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) over enterprise knowledge stored in Google Cloud Storage.

Initial implementations relied exclusively on semantic vector search using Qdrant. While semantic retrieval performs well for natural language queries, it is less effective for exact keyword searches involving:

- Product codes
- Error messages
- Document identifiers
- File names
- Version numbers
- Acronyms
- Technical terminology
- JSON keys
- Excel cell values

Enterprise users frequently search using exact identifiers rather than natural language.

A complementary lexical search engine was required.

---

# Decision

The platform will use **OpenSearch** as the lexical search engine.

OpenSearch indexes document text using the BM25 ranking algorithm and provides high-performance keyword search.

Hybrid Search combines:

- Semantic Search (Qdrant)
- Lexical Search (OpenSearch)

Candidate results are merged before reranking.

---

# Alternatives Considered

## Elasticsearch

Pros

- Mature ecosystem
- Rich feature set
- Excellent performance

Cons

- License restrictions
- Less attractive for open-source projects

Decision

Not selected.

---

## PostgreSQL Full-Text Search

Pros

- No additional infrastructure
- Good for small datasets

Cons

- Limited scalability
- Less flexible ranking
- Not optimized for large enterprise document collections

Decision

Not selected.

---

## Apache Solr

Pros

- Mature
- Feature rich

Cons

- Operational complexity
- Smaller community compared to OpenSearch

Decision

Not selected.

---

## Typesense / Meilisearch

Pros

- Simple deployment
- Fast search

Cons

- Limited enterprise ecosystem
- Fewer advanced search capabilities

Decision

Not selected.

---

# Rationale

OpenSearch was selected because it:

- Implements BM25 ranking
- Supports enterprise-scale indexing
- Scales horizontally
- Provides rich filtering capabilities
- Supports structured metadata
- Integrates well with Hybrid Search
- Is fully open source
- Has a mature ecosystem

---

# Consequences

Positive

- Improved exact keyword retrieval
- Better enterprise search quality
- Better support for structured documents
- Improved RAG grounding
- Higher retrieval recall

Negative

- Additional infrastructure
- Separate indexing pipeline
- Additional operational monitoring
- Increased storage requirements

